"## Reproduction attempt: the bug does **not** reproduce with well-formed CSV\n\n**Case 1 — the exact reported scenario (double-quoted field with a comma):**\n\n`bug_report.csv`:\n```\nname,age,note\n\"Doe, Jane\",30,\"  keeps spaces  \"\n```\nTool output:\n```\nTotal rows: 1\nTotal columns: 3\nFirst data row: ['Doe, Jane', '30', '  keeps spaces  ']\n```\n✅ Correct — the quoted field stays intact as one column.\n\nHowever, I did find the **reported symptom** in two cases — both of which are *non-standard* CSV input:\n\n**Case 2 — single quotes instead of double quotes** (`bug_singlequote.csv`):\n```\nname,age,note\n'Single, Jane',30,ok\n```\n```\nTotal columns: 4\nFirst data row: [\"'Single\", \" Jane'\", '30', 'ok']\n```\n❌ Symptom reproduced: the field splits into 2 columns.\n\n**Case 3 — space before the opening quote** (`bug_spacequote.csv`):\n```\nname,age,note\n \"Space, Jane\",30,ok\n```\n```\nTotal columns: 4\nFirst data row: [' \"Space', ' Jane\"', '30', 'ok']\n```\n❌ Symptom reproduced: the field splits into 2 columns.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe reported bug **does not reproduce with valid CSV** (double quotes at field start). In cases 2 and 3 the tool is actually behaving **correctly per RFC 4180** and Python's `csv` module semantics:\n\n- Only `\"` at the *very first character* of a field is a quote character.\n- Single quotes are literal text — `'Single, Jane'` is genuinely two unquoted fields.\n- A quote preceded by whitespace is literal — so ` \"Space, Jane\"` is genuinely two fields.\n\nThe reporter's file most likely contains single-quoted \"fields\" (common in exports from some spreadsheets/databases) or stray whitespace before opening quotes.\n\nTo resolve this properly, I'd suggest one of:\n1. **Get the user's actual file** (or its exact bytes) to confirm which case they hit.\n2. If leniency is desired, the tool could accept options like `skipinitialspace=True` (fixes case 3) or a configurable quote character (fixes case 2) — but that's a behavior change, so I haven't made it without your go-ahead.\n\nWant me to add either of those as opt-in CLI flags?"